kaggle-detector-retrain/upload/

# P3 detector cost probes — regenerable in seconds by
#   export-p3-graphs.py --cost_imgsz 640 512 416
# and they carry no accuracy meaning (no tokens were ever built at those sizes), so there is no
# reason to put 114 MB of them in history. The three real P3 graphs are NOT ignored.
deployment/export/p3-detector-fp32-imgsz*.onnx

# external dataset (1.29 GB, not versioned)
data/isafetybench/

# Detector sweep checkpoints under runs/ — the `sweep_results/**/*.pt` rule above contains a
# slash, so git anchors it to the repo root and it does NOT match runs/detect/sweep_results/.
# 146 MB of YOLO best.pt/last.pt; best.pt is referenced by
# tools/count-actors-in-contradictory-clips.py, so the files stay on disk, just out of history.
runs/**/weights/*.pt

# R48 lane: the CLIP ViT-B/32 vision tower exports at 351 MB fp32, over GitHub's 100 MB hard
# per-file limit -- a push carrying it would be rejected outright. Every other graph in
# deployment/export/ is 48 MB or under and stays tracked. Rebuild it with
# deployment/scripts/export-clip-vision-tower.py; the weights come from the HF cache, not this repo.
deployment/export/r48-clip-vit-b32-vision-fp32.onnx
deployment/export/r48-clip-vit-b32-vision-fp32.onnx.data
# Round-3 tower exports, same treatment and same reason. siglip2B16 is 355 MB and clipB16 329 MB,
# both over GitHub's 100 MB hard per-file limit; mobileclip_s1 is 82 MB, under the limit but well
# over the 48 MB ceiling every other tracked graph in this directory respects. All three are
# rebuilt by deployment/scripts/export-round3-towers-onnx.py, which re-runs its own torch-vs-ONNX
# parity gate (rel <= 1e-4) and refuses to leave a graph behind that fails it; the weights come
# from the HF / open_clip cache, not this repo. The host parity verdicts that these graphs were
# accepted under are recorded in deployment/JETSON-RUNBOOK-2026-10-19.md, which IS tracked.
deployment/export/r48-tower-*.onnx
# Detector checkpoint, same treatment as yolo11s.pt above.
yolo26s.pt

# TensorRT engines are device-specific -- an engine built on this Orin Nano is not loadable on
# another board (the deserialiser warns and the plan is rebuilt), so they carry no value in
# history, and the CLIP tower engine alone is 174 MB, over GitHub's 100 MB per-file limit.
# deployment/export/jetson-p3/ and jetson-r48/ hold per-pipeline symlink subsets because the
# shared jetson/ dir mixes engine build dates; all of them are rebuilt on the device.
deployment/export/jetson/
deployment/export/jetson-*/
*.engine

# Post-hoc sensitivity analysis outputs. The `sweep_results/**/*.json` rule above would swallow
# these two, but they are not per-clip prediction dumps -- they are the summary tables that the
# section 4.5.7 write-up cites directly (margins against the 4-thread control with their
# source-unit bootstrap CIs, and the asymmetric-measure table). Keep them.
!sweep_results/posthoc_sensitivity/paired_vs_control.json
!sweep_results/posthoc_sensitivity/asymmetric_measures.json
# The round-3 tower verdict. Eight towers, Benjamini-Hochberg over their eight p-values, and the
# conclusion that no tower clears rank-1 threshold 0.00625 -- i.e. the central negative result of
# the round. The blanket sweep_results rule above was hiding it, which would have left the verdict
# out of the repository while every input to it was in.
!sweep_results/posthoc_sensitivity/round3_tower_bh.json

# Demo renders behind Hinh 5.3 / Hinh 5.4. The annotated MP4s are regenerated by
# demo/run-unsafe-behavior-demo.py; the per-frame probability timelines next to them are the
# actual data those two figures plot, are a few KB each, and stay tracked.
demo/out/*.mp4
